<!--
  Minimal, and it stays minimal.

  One entitlement: this app makes outbound HTTPS requests to a control plane
  somebody chose. It reaches nothing else.

  Not here, deliberately: there is no App Sandbox, so `com.apple.security.app-sandbox`
  is absent and keychain access needs no entitlement of its own. The hardened
  runtime is on (`bundle.macOS.hardenedRuntime`), which is what notarization
  requires, and WKWebView runs out of process carrying its own.

  This file becomes load-bearing the day sandboxing or App Store distribution is
  wanted, and not one day before. `docs/NATIVE.md` carries what that would take.

  ⚠ **There is a second entitlements file beside this one and it is not a
  duplicate.** `entitlements-node.plist` belongs to
  `Contents/Helpers/Reemoat Runtime.app`, the helper holding the Node runtime the
  daemon runs under, which needs five hardened-runtime exceptions that V8 cannot
  start without. They are deliberately *not* merged in here: this is the
  signature on the window holding the fleet's credential, and
  `disable-library-validation` on it would mean any dylib could be loaded into
  that process. Two bundles, two signatures, two sets — which is how the hardened
  runtime is meant to be used, and the reason that file exists rather than this
  one growing. `build-daemon.mjs` signs the helper with it; the bundler signs this
  app with this one, and seals the signed helper inside.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
